uestion/math-academy-id "q-112466" :question/is-example false :question/problem "The equation ${y}^{2}+4y-9=0$ can be written in the form $(y+p{)}^{2}=q.$   Find $p+q.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side and complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+4y-9 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}+4y & =9 \\\\ {y}^{2}+2\\cdot 2\\cdot y & =9 \\\\ {y}^{2}+2\\cdot 2\\cdot y+{2}^{2}-{2}^{2} & =9 \\\\ (y+2{)}^{2}-4 & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+2{)}^{2}-4 & =9 \\\\ (y+2{)}^{2} & =13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $p=2,$ $q=13,$ and $p+q=15.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-112466/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7f04ab7-f6f6-5d01-bfcf-b667e37e4f0b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-112466"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-3435"] :question/worked-solution "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, the $-10z$ term to the left-hand side, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2}-50 & =-10z \\\\ {z}^{2}+10z & =50 \\\\ {z}^{2}+2\\cdot 5\\cdot z & =50 \\\\ {z}^{2}+2\\cdot 5\\cdot z+{5}^{2}-{5}^{2} & =50 \\\\ (z+5{)}^{2}-{5}^{2} & =50\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(z+5{)}^{2}-{5}^{2} & =50 \\\\ (z+5{)}^{2} & =75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $p=5,q=75,$ and $p+q=80.$\n\nNext, we solve the equation using the square-root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(z+5{)}^{2} & =75 \\\\ \\sqrt{(z+5{)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{75} \\\\ |z+5| & =\\sqrt{75} \\\\ z+5 & =\\pm 5\\sqrt{3} \\\\ z & =-5\\pm 5\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-112472/selection/bf5ce874dbbd55366cbdfdec21a896ffbb2f89de05e1ebe2c86a8dec225a7e81" :answer/id #uuid "68d9f04e-5ae7-5f9c-8782-1c18150366d0" :a